// expect-error: NITPICK-TYPE-087
//
// PROBE 19 — AN OWNING TYPE CANNOT CLAIM `Copy`. The claim `vec_at`'s bound
// rests on (cycle 0.1.3c, TM-194, and since cycle 0.2.0a TM-211; `SAFETY.md`
// S-18h).
//
// SINCE CYCLE 0.2.0a THE BOUND IS THE PRELUDE'S `Copy` (the compiler's D-327)
// and this file asks it: `impl:string:Copy = { };` is `NITPICK-TYPE-087`, once,
// at the impl -- *"`string` owns what it holds, so a copy of it would be a
// second owner"* -- measured at `5fbaf4a`. Until then the bound was this
// library's `Pod` and the question below was `Pod`'s; its answer stands as
// the record of it, and the file keeps its name.
//
// ASKS: may a consumer declare `string` a `Pod`, writing `pod_copy` as the
// trait declares it -- `self` lent, `pass self`?
//
// MATTERS BECAUSE `vec_at<T: Pod>` hands an element back by value, and the
// bound is worth something only if the LANGUAGE decides who may implement it.
// If this compiled, `vec_at` at `T = string` would hand back a second owner of
// the element -- the destructive read `defect/generic_owning_copy/case5`
// measured, turned into a double free.
//
// ANSWER, measured at `c970483` and `c3bdae2`: NO. `pass self` of a lent owner
// is `NITPICK-TYPE-047` -- "this parameter was lent, not given" -- exactly once.
// THE REFUSAL IS THE ASSERTION (B-7). The positive twin is
// `../unit/vec_at_pod.npk`: a type that owns nothing implements it in one line.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT STOP, and says so: `TYPE-047`'s message suggests declaring
// the parameter `move`, and at `c970483` an impl that does is ACCEPTED, though
// the trait lends it -- the compiler's DEF-116, fixed at no pin of ours. Such
// an impl makes `vec_at` a double free (measured, `meta/roadmap/done/0.1/0.1.3c.md`
// §1.6). This library writes none; `nitpick-regex` pins the shape. (CLOSED
// at `5fbaf4a`: such an impl is `NITPICK-TYPE-014` there, the compiler's
// landing 69, and `Copy` has no method to declare one on -- TM-211.)
//
// *(CYCLE 0.4.0a, THE ADOPTION OF COMPILER `2e1cb4a` -- 2026-10-22, TM-263:
// THE TYPE ASKED ABOUT IS THIS LIBRARY'S OWN `Bytes`, WHERE IT WAS `string`.
// Since that compiler an impl names a type or a trait declared in its own
// manifest's tree, or it is `NITPICK-TYPE-099` at the impl, asked before the
// `Copy` judgement (the compiler's D-359, landing 124). `impl:string:Copy`
// names neither -- `string` is a builtin and `Copy` the prelude's -- so this
// file would have read the orphan rule and not the judgement S-18h rests on.
// `Bytes` is declared in this tree and owns a `buffer`, so the orphan rule
// admits the impl and the judgement refuses it: `NITPICK-TYPE-087`, once, at
// the impl -- *"`Bytes` owns what it holds, so a copy of it would be a second
// owner"* -- at both compilers, measured. Were it admitted, `vec_at` at
// `T = Bytes` would hand back a second owner of a sink's body. `string`'s
// claim is `probe19b`'s, from the commit that moves the pin, where the
// orphan rule refuses it before the judgement is asked.)*
mod:probe19_pod_owner_refused;

use "../../src/core/vec.npk".*;
use "../../src/core/bytes.npk".Bytes;

impl:Bytes:Copy = { };

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
    exit 0i32;
};

// A SUPERSET of what `NITPICK-REACH-002` asks, deliberately (`SAFETY.md` S-6b,
// constraint 3: a superset compiles). If a later compiler reordered its phases
// so that the reach analysis ran before the check this file asserts, a
// failsafe naming exactly the demanded arms could add a stray `REACH-002` to
// the refusal; this one cannot.
func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest)    { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)           { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)       { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)       { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)       { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)          { exit 96i32; },
        (DivByZero)         { exit 97i32; },
        (DivOverflow)       { exit 98i32; },
        (StackExhausted)    { exit 106i32; },
        (MachineFault)      { exit 107i32; },
        (DecreasesViolated) { exit 108i32; },
        (LimitViolated)     { exit 109i32; },
        (*)                 { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
